<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Amago clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1486 CE – 1566 CE (approx.) · capital Gassantoda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Amago of Izumo, who rose from deputies to masters of much of western Honshu before falling to the Mori in 1566.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Amago were deputy governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province">Izumo</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyogoku-clan">Kyogoku</a> family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-tsunehisa">Amago Tsunehisa</a> was expelled from his post but, according to tradition, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gassantoda-castle">Gassan-Toda</a> castle by a ruse in 1486, and went on to make himself independent ruler of Izumo.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-tsunehisa">Tsunehisa</a> and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-haruhisa">Haruhisa</a> extended their power over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanin-region">San'in</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanyo-region">San'yo</a> regions, at their height claiming influence over eleven provinces. They contested the Iwami <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mine with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ouchi</a> and withstood an Ouchi siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gassantoda-castle">Gassan-Toda</a> in 1542 and 1543. The mountain fortress was the heart of a network of allied local lords.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-haruhisa">Haruhisa</a>'s death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-motonari">Mori Motonari</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province">Izumo</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gassantoda-castle">Gassan-Toda</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-yoshihisa">Amago Yoshihisa</a> surrendered in 1566. The retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamanaka-yukimori">Yamanaka Yukimori</a> fought to restore the clan until his death in 1578.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/amago-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Amago clan domain" width="130" height="130"><figcaption>Image: 俊 (Shun Toshi) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamon_yotumeyui.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1486 CE – 1566 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gassantoda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amago Tsunehisa, Amago Haruhisa, Amago Yoshihisa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Amago clan, Amago of Izumo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=amago-clan-domain" aria-label="Amago clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8163 877 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Amago clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8483 1133h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8483 1133h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=amago-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

